"scenario": {
"title": "Zara's Hoopla Countdown",
"cast": [
{
"name": "Zara",
"emoji": "🧑"
},
{
"name": "Mr Kemp",
"emoji": "🧑"
},
{
"name": "Ms Obi",
"emoji": "🧑"
},
{
"name": "Britney",
"emoji": "🐔"
}
],
"intro": "Zara, a trainee solicitor; Mr Kemp, the friendly hoopla stallholder; the defendant company Brightside Trading Ltd and its solicitor, Ms Obi. Zara is serving her very first claim form, on Brightside Trading Ltd. Mr Kemp has handed her a hoop, and the stall's big clock has started ticking...",
"steps": [
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Zara",
"text": "The court issued my claim form on 10 March. How long have I got?"
}
],
"q": "By when must Zara take the step required to serve it in England?",
"opts": [
"Before midnight on 10 July.",
"Before midnight on 10 June.",
"Before midnight on 10 September.",
"It must be deemed served by 10 July."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "The step must be taken before 12 midnight on the calendar day 4 months after issue (CPR 7.5).",
"whys": [
"",
"It is 4 months, not 3.",
"6 months is for serving outside the jurisdiction.",
"It is the step that must be taken in time, not deemed service."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Zara",
"text": "Brightside's email address is on their letterhead. I'll just email it!"
},
{
"who": "Mr Kemp",
"text": "Careful. Mr Barton emailed his claim form on the last day without a written yes, and lost his whole claim."
},
{
"who": "Britney",
"text": "A letterhead email isn't a yes!"
}
],
"q": "Can Zara serve the claim form by email?",
"opts": [
"Only if Brightside (or its solicitor) has previously indicated in writing that it will accept service by email and given the address.",
"Yes. An email address on the letterhead is enough.",
"Yes, as long as Brightside actually reads it.",
"Yes, because she is only a trainee."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "PD 6A para 4.1: a letterhead email address is not enough.",
"whys": [
"",
"Not unless it says it may be used for service.",
"The defendant's knowledge of the claim is not enough.",
"There's no special leniency, not even for litigants in person."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Ms Obi",
"text": "(by letter) I confirm that I am authorised to accept service of the claim form on behalf of Brightside Trading Ltd."
}
],
"q": "Where must Zara now serve the claim form?",
"opts": [
"On Ms Obi, the solicitor.",
"On Brightside at its registered office.",
"By handing it to a Brightside director personally, to be safe.",
"Anywhere she likes."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "Once a solicitor has said in writing it is authorised to accept service, the claim form must be served on the solicitor (CPR 6.7).",
"whys": [
"",
"Not once a solicitor has been nominated.",
"Serving the defendant personally is then invalid.",
"The rules say the solicitor."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Zara",
"text": "I posted it first class to Ms Obi on 9 July. Deemed service will be after 10 July. Have I missed it?"
}
],
"q": "Is service in time?",
"opts": [
"Yes. The step (posting) was completed before the deadline, even though deemed service falls later.",
"No. Deemed service falls after 10 July.",
"Only if it arrives by 10 July.",
"No, she needed the court's permission to post it."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "For a claim form, the claimant beats the 4-month deadline by completing the step in time.",
"whys": [
"",
"Deemed service does not matter for the deadline.",
"Posting is the step required.",
"First class post is a permitted method."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Zara",
"text": "Oh no. I named \"Brightside Ltd\" on the claim form, but the contract was with Brightside Trading Ltd. And limitation has now expired!"
}
],
"q": "Can the court let Zara swap the name?",
"opts": [
"Yes. The limitation period was current when the claim was started, and the right company is being substituted for one named by mistake.",
"No. Parties can never be changed after limitation expires.",
"Only if Brightside Trading Ltd consents in writing.",
"Only if the claim form hasn't been served yet."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "CPR 19.6 and Limitation Act 1980 s.35.",
"whys": [
"",
"They can if the addition or substitution is necessary, e.g. a mistake.",
"Written consent is needed to add a claimant, not a defendant.",
"After service the court's permission is needed, but substitution is still possible."
]
}
],
"outro": {
"who": "Britney",
"text": "Remember Mr Kemp's story: a claim form emailed without a written yes to email service can't be rescued, even if the other side knew all about it."
},
"icon": "",
"label": "Scenario: Zara's Hoopla Countdown"
},
